[Music]  hey everyone and welcome to lesson one  of the basics of data visualization with  r for ecologists  i'm really excited to get started so  let's go right in we're going to cover  the very basics of creating data  visualizations  using the base r package and that's the  package that comes pre-installed with  r if you've already taken my basics of  our free colleges course  and feel confident with the basics then  you might want to skip this module  but either way i think it should offer a  good refresher  so first let's go ahead and load the  data now for this course we're going to  be using mostly built-in data  using the data function to access a  variety of different data  sets that come built in with r and  that's just to make things a bit easier  so we can get right to visualization  so first we're going to load this data  set called  plant growth and so when we run this  line of code  we get this thing that pops up here in  the environment it says promise when we  click on it or whenever we use this  plant growth data frame  the then this gets converted into the  data frame that it is and  so let's take a look at what these data  look like  so if we scroll up here in the console  we can see that we have one column for  weight  and one column called group and so in  this case  for these data we have a total of 30  data points  and weight represents the dry biomass of  each plant in grams  after it's been collected and dried of  course so if we do  table plant growth  and run that actually we want to do the  do a table of all the different groups  we can see that we have  10 different plants for each of these  treatments but let's say that it turns  out that we have another column in this  data set  on the amount of water that each plant  has received throughout its life  in liters so here are the data  they're already in this file so you  don't have to enter them yourself  and we're just going to add that to this  vector of water  and then add that vector of water as a  new column to the plant growth data set  so great now we have these three columns  to work with  so now for your first plot let's create  a scatter plot to see how plant weight  varies  as a function of how much water the  plants received  and to do that all you have to use is  the plot function  and the first argument is the x  in this case we're going to do plant  growth  and remember the dollar sign is used to  select particular columns so in this  case  water so if we were to run just this we  get the vector of water values  so that's going to be the x-axis the  y-axis is going to be the data frame  plant growth  but this time we're going to select  weight so now we can see  this vector of weights so when we run  this  and there you go we get a scatter plot  with plant weight on the y-axis as we  defined it here and plant water or  excuse me the amount of water that was  given to each plant on the x-axis here  and we get this scatter plot so congrats  this is your first plot  and you can make this plot smaller or  larger by resizing this window  that's the great thing about rstudio is  it just has all this built-in  functionality to help you visualize and  work with data in this way  all right but there's some other ways in  which we can use the plot function  we created this plot using the plot  function and specifying the x and the y  axis but there is another way to use the  plot function that is a bit of a better  practice and it's easier to specify the  x and y  axes by saying y is a function of  x or y and then this  tilde symbol which is above underneath  your escape key usually you have to hold  shift down and then press it it's this  horizontal squiggly line  i think it's called a tilde or a tilde  or something like that either way in r  it represents  is a function of  so you can oops whenever you see that  tilde you can read it as  is a function of so in this case y is a  function of  x and so here we can say the weight of  each plant  is the y-axis so the weight of each  plant is a function  of the amount of water that it received  so to use this with the plot function  all we have to do is plot  plant growth  weight so here notice we're reversing  these because we're doing the y first  as a function of plant and as i'm typing  it you'll see this pops up so you can  just click that to make it a little  faster  so plant weight is a function of the  amount of water that it was that it  received  since if we run this you'll see that  nothing changes here then that's just  because it plotted the exact same thing  over again it's just another way of  using the plot function  but this type of notation is really  helpful because we can actually add an  argument to this function  to indicate where the data come from  so instead of having to continuously  write  plant growth here we can just modify  this by saying  weight is a function of water  and then add an argument for data so  data equals  plant growth so now if we run this again  again you'll see that almost nothing  changes here except  now the x and the y axis are just the  name of the variable  instead of this if we click on this  arrow here we can go to the previous  plot we had  instead of having plant growth dollar  sign weight  we just have weight because that's the  name of the column and  it knows where to get those data from  because we added in this data argument  data equals plant growth great so that's  it for this  intro lesson quick summary we loaded our  data  added another column to the data set  plotted a very simple scatter plot using  the plot function  and i showed you a neater way of using  the plot function  by saying the y-axis is a function of  the x-axis  and then adding in the data argument in  the next few lessons we're going to go  over a bit more about how to plot the  two most common types of plots in  ecology using r's built  in base package and then later we'll  move on to a more advanced plotting  system but for now thanks for watching  hope you enjoyed this  and see you in the next lesson  you

